# An import that names a file with the file name left off — refused, with the
# file named — and then the fix applied by the two tools that carry a
# diagnostic's own bytes.
#
# The point of *this* repository is that the refusal cannot be read off the
# string. `lib/money/test/cents.buri` writes `from "//lib/money/testing"` and
# is correct; `lib/money/parse.buri` writes `from "//lib/money/cents"` and is
# refused. Same shape, same package, one segment each — and the difference is
# that `lib/money/testing/` holds a surface and `lib/money/cents.buri` is a
# file. Only the layout says which, so only the resolver can say which.
#
# The suite beside them is the other half of the same fact: a surface is named
# as a module wherever the import is written, so `//lib/money` is what
# `cmd/app/main.buri` writes for it and it is what the library's own suite
# writes for it too.
#
# The second refusal ends at a different file. A binary's entry point is not a
# surface — `//cmd/app` would be that package's `lib.buri` and there is not one
# — so `//cmd/app/main` is refused with `//cmd/app/main.buri`, and the two
# fixes in this repository land on two files that no rewriting of the text
# could have found.
#
# The last steps are the fixes being *acted on*, because a suggestion nobody
# can act on is not a fix. `textDocument/codeAction` is what an editor offers —
# the same `edits` the diagnostic carries, replayed verbatim — and `buri lint
# --fix` is the terminal's half of it. Then the build and the suites all go
# green without a hand edit anywhere in the case.
#
# Schema: the repository-case manifest, cli/tests/harness/case.rs. Textproto,
# but not a Buri file: only repo/ below is a Buri repository.

doc: "an import that leaves a file name off is refused, the diagnostic names the file it meant, and both the editor and lint --fix write it"

run {
  args: ["build", "//..."]
  exit: 1
  golden: "build.txt"
}

run {
  args: ["build", "//...", "--error-format=json", "--force"]
  exit: 1
  golden: "build.json"
}

# The binary's suite, whose entry point is a file and not a surface.
run {
  args: ["test", "//cmd/app", "--force"]
  exit: 1
  golden: "test.txt"
}

# What an editor offers on the line: one quick fix, carrying the exact bytes.
run {
  args: ["lsp"]
  exit: 0
  stdin: "session.jsonl"
  stream: OUT
  golden: "session.txt"
}

# And the terminal's half of the same edits, applied — both of them, in one
# pass.
run {
  args: ["lint", "--fix", "//..."]
  exit: 0
  golden: "fix.txt"
}

run {
  args: ["build", "//..."]
  exit: 0
}

run {
  args: ["test", "//..."]
  exit: 0
  golden: "test-green.txt"
  stream: OUT
}
